WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.734 --> 00:00:03.135
GRADY POWELL: This is our
second chance tournament.

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:05.205
Tonight, the dueling begins.

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:07.774
And up first, the
Tough Breaks duel

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:10.543
where two competitors who
suffered catastrophic failures

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:11.811
return to the forge.

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.179 line:14%
 Name's James Mays.

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:14.514 line:14%
I'm 41.

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:17.917
I was eliminated last time
because I was repairing work

00:00:17.983 --> 00:00:20.253
and ended up breaking my blade.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:20.787
 [grunts]

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:21.687
 Oh!

00:00:21.754 --> 00:00:23.389
 I knew it.

00:00:23.489 --> 00:00:25.291
JAMES: I'm tremendously excited
to be in the second chance

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:27.560
tournament because it
gives me the opportunity

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:30.329
to prove to myself and
my family and friends

00:00:30.396 --> 00:00:35.734
that I am worthy of being a
"Forged in Fire" champion.

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:36.769 line:14%
 My name's Steve Wells.

00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:38.638 line:14%
I'm 48 years old.

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:41.207 line:8%
Last time I was here, my
blade broke because I missed

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:44.177
my normalization process
in the heat treat,

00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:46.212
and I want to redeem that.

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:48.882
It's exhilarating being
back in the forge.

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.449
I guess I'm kind of
an adrenaline junkie.

00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:56.155
And since I'm too old to ride
bulls, I guess this is my fix.

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:58.357 line:8%
 Gentlemen, welcome
to the forge.

00:00:58.457 --> 00:01:00.560
You guys are here for
a chance to redeem

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.562
yourselves and go for that title
of "Forged in Fire" Champion.

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:06.165
But just because we're
giving you a second chance,

00:01:06.231 --> 00:01:08.300
does not mean we're
going to go easy on you.

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.804
Today, we want you to
build blades out of these.

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:20.045
Bladesmiths, this is a pile
of blades that suffered

00:01:20.112 --> 00:01:21.714
the same fate as yours.

00:01:21.814 --> 00:01:25.051
They broke during testing and
they broke during a build.

00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:27.119
We want you guys to
build signature blades

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.255
in your signature
style between 12

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:34.294
and 14 inches using at least
three pieces of this pile.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.629
STEVE: What have I got to
deal with in these blades?

00:01:36.696 --> 00:01:38.497
Something was wrong with
them in the beginning,

00:01:38.564 --> 00:01:42.068
so I've got to find a
way to break them down

00:01:42.134 --> 00:01:43.169
and put them into a new blade.

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:44.336
 All right, gentlemen.

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:45.871
You have five
hours on the clock,

00:01:45.938 --> 00:01:50.043
and your time starts now.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.478
STEVE: Looks like the
table of J's broken blades

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:53.780
of his dreams.

00:01:53.846 --> 00:01:57.283
JAMES: I've got one tang
with about 4 inches of blade,

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:58.885
another tang with
about an inch of blade,

00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:04.857
and then a third piece that is
about six inches of just blade.

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:06.125
JUDGE: It really
looks like James

00:02:06.225 --> 00:02:08.027
is going to try and do a stack.

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:09.662
JUDGE: There's just
so many variables

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:11.831
when you're stacking a
bunch of mystery metal

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:12.965
and trying to get a weld.

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:14.266
 I've got my billet
welded together,

00:02:14.366 --> 00:02:16.402
and I'm starting to draw it out.

00:02:16.502 --> 00:02:19.438
Having a thin blade concerns
me a bit because you can break

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:21.007
a blade during
the testing stage,

00:02:21.074 --> 00:02:24.277
and I would hate to see
that happen again today.

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:25.077
JUDGE: All right, James.

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:27.713
It's quenched.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.549
 [bleep]

00:02:29.615 --> 00:02:31.651
 I could see the
warping up from here.

00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:35.321
 I want to address that warp
before we get any further,

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:37.323
but I don't want to
snap my blade again.

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:38.791
GRADY POWELL: Don't
yank on it, man.

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:43.829
 Oh yeah.

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:46.799
I feel like it's straight enough
that I can grind the rest down,

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:49.569
and I feel like everything's
going good at this point.

00:02:51.771 --> 00:02:54.606 line:8%
I get my handle traced, I run
it over to the tempering oven,

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:57.276 line:8%
put it in there, and start
the tempering process

00:02:57.376 --> 00:03:00.079 line:8%
and go back to working
on my handle material.

00:03:00.179 --> 00:03:02.615
I make my three-part
panel material,

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:04.984
and I've got it
setting in a curing.

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:07.987
It's time to go back
to work on my blade.

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:12.391
Oh, you gotta be
[bleep] kidding me.

00:03:12.491 --> 00:03:14.226
JUDGE: Oh, he's got
a warp going on.

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:16.129
JAMES: I'm afraid to
start pulling and bending

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:20.833
on this one, so all I can
do is soften the spine

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.234
and straighten the blade.

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:24.437
I work on it the most I
can, and I get to a point

00:03:24.503 --> 00:03:26.439
where I have to just go with it.

00:03:28.774 --> 00:03:31.077
 There's steel in there
I definitely don't want.

00:03:31.143 --> 00:03:33.479
I see some really
bad delaminations,

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:35.414
so I got to make
another canister.

00:03:35.514 --> 00:03:37.316
I can't see another
way to do it.

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:38.417
I'm a little worried
about getting

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:40.453
a weld good tight
fit inside that,

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:42.488
but I think I'll have
plenty of material.

00:03:42.588 --> 00:03:45.458
I don't think it's going to
be a lack of material problem.

00:03:45.524 --> 00:03:47.660
Well, that took a lot of time.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.528
So while this
can's in the forge,

00:03:49.628 --> 00:03:52.498
I'm going to do a
full-tang handle.

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:53.165
JUDGE: Oh, yeah.

00:03:53.265 --> 00:03:54.099
Now, that-- that's hot.

00:03:54.199 --> 00:03:55.768
JUDGE: Beautiful.

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:57.570
 I'm feeling pretty
good about the can.

00:03:57.637 --> 00:04:01.173
I think everything's good, so
I've got to quench this blade.

00:04:01.273 --> 00:04:02.141
GRADY POWELL: All right.

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:03.609
We've got another quench.

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:04.410
 Perfect.

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:05.811
 Nice.

00:04:05.911 --> 00:04:06.712
 Looks good.

00:04:06.812 --> 00:04:08.181
Everything's straight.

00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:09.515
GRADY POWELL: Steve's got
his blade in his vise.

00:04:09.581 --> 00:04:11.751
Looks like he's about
to torch temper.

00:04:11.817 --> 00:04:13.853
STEVE: If I get too
much heat in this thing,

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:15.221
it's going to be too soft.

00:04:15.287 --> 00:04:16.756
It's not going to hold an edge.

00:04:16.856 --> 00:04:20.626
If I don't go enough, it's
going to be hard and brittle.

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:23.228
I'm spending my last few
minutes finishing my handle

00:04:23.295 --> 00:04:25.131
and getting some
weight off my blade.

00:04:25.198 --> 00:04:27.933
And I've still got to
get in the acid edge.

00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:30.469
Too damn heavy.

00:04:30.569 --> 00:04:32.204
GRADY POWELL: That's
something I haven't seen yet.

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:35.041 line:8%
James is stropping
on his leather boots.

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:36.909 line:8%
JAMES: I pull in a
little bit too much

00:04:36.976 --> 00:04:38.611
and start shaving my boot.

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:40.513
If I'm fortunate enough
to win the competition,

00:04:40.579 --> 00:04:43.081
I think I'll have the
money for new boots.

00:04:43.148 --> 00:04:47.553
GRADY POWELL: 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.

00:04:47.653 --> 00:04:50.022
Gentlemen, this round is over.

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:53.326
J: All right, James, Steve,
welcome to our Tough Breaks

00:04:53.392 --> 00:04:56.028
strength test, the antler chop.

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:59.165
Now, I'm gonna take your blades
and beat them mercilessly

00:04:59.232 --> 00:05:00.800
on these antlers
to test your edge

00:05:00.866 --> 00:05:04.336
retention as well as the overall
construction of your blades.

00:05:04.436 --> 00:05:05.771
Now, what your blades
do to the antlers

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:08.441
is second to what the
antlers to to your blades.

00:05:08.507 --> 00:05:10.810
Both you guys had blades
break last time you were here,

00:05:10.876 --> 00:05:13.846
and let's hope that
doesn't happen again.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:15.081
James, are you ready?

00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:15.848
 Let's do it.

00:05:15.915 --> 00:05:16.616
 OK.

00:05:16.683 --> 00:05:17.549
Let's go for it.

00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:19.685
 I hope J don't
snap my blade again.

00:05:19.752 --> 00:05:22.588
[music playing]

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:31.263
 [grunting]

00:05:31.363 --> 00:05:34.834
 [sighs] [bleep], man.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:35.734
 All right, James.

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:36.702
You survived.

00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:38.004
Good job.

00:05:38.104 --> 00:05:41.540
You took broken blades and
made it into something that

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:43.175
can survive an antler chop.

00:05:43.241 --> 00:05:45.110
I'll start off with your handle.

00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:47.747
You do have the
contouring there.

00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:49.481
That helps a lot,
but it feels like it

00:05:49.581 --> 00:05:51.550
tapers down towards the back.

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.654
You do have a slight
kick to the left now,

00:05:55.721 --> 00:05:59.725
and you've got some chipping,
but it didn't break.

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:00.292
Good job.

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:01.293
 Thank you.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:02.194
 Hey, Steve.

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:03.262
You ready to do this?

00:06:03.329 --> 00:06:03.996
 Does it matter?

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:04.997
 No.

00:06:05.097 --> 00:06:05.764
We're going to do it anyway.

00:06:05.865 --> 00:06:09.735
[music playing]

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:20.880
[grunting]

00:06:20.946 --> 00:06:21.414
All right, Steve.

00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:22.748
You survived.

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:23.783
Great job.

00:06:23.849 --> 00:06:25.785
Everything's tight
and nothing moved,

00:06:25.851 --> 00:06:28.454
but you do have a
bunch of chipping

00:06:28.554 --> 00:06:30.857
and rolling right here.

00:06:30.957 --> 00:06:32.625
Your handle's very blocky.

00:06:32.725 --> 00:06:35.761
Rounding these corners off would
have made it more comfortable.

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:37.263
And the other thing
is there's just

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:39.865
a lot of weight on this
thing, but everything

00:06:39.965 --> 00:06:40.766
else is right and tight.

00:06:40.833 --> 00:06:41.333
Good job.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.168
 Thank you.

00:06:45.304 --> 00:06:46.439
 All right, bladesmiths.

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:48.407
Your blades made
out of broken blades

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:51.377
survived a very brutal
test by a judge who's

00:06:51.443 --> 00:06:53.012
notorious for breaking blades.

00:06:53.079 --> 00:06:55.647
But we need to find out
how sharp your blades are.

00:06:55.714 --> 00:06:58.951
This is a sharpness test,
the pork loin slice.

00:06:59.018 --> 00:07:00.552
To find out how sharp
your weapons are,

00:07:00.619 --> 00:07:02.154
I'm going to take your
weapons to try to cut

00:07:02.254 --> 00:07:03.856
through these pork loins.

00:07:03.923 --> 00:07:04.857
James, you're up first.

00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:05.925
Are you ready for this?

00:07:06.025 --> 00:07:06.825
- Let's go.
- All right.

00:07:06.926 --> 00:07:07.760
Let's do this.

00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:16.102
[music playing]

00:07:23.141 --> 00:07:24.109
All right, James.

00:07:24.176 --> 00:07:25.377
Let's talk about your blade.

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:28.380
First up, there's a
nice balance to it.

00:07:28.447 --> 00:07:29.982
It's easy to wield.

00:07:30.049 --> 00:07:33.185
Your handle construction
is on the smooth side,

00:07:33.252 --> 00:07:37.089
but cutting through these pork
loins was not hard at all.

00:07:37.155 --> 00:07:38.824
Your weapon, sir, it will cut.

00:07:38.924 --> 00:07:39.992
 Thank you.

00:07:40.059 --> 00:07:41.427
 All right, Steve, your turn.
So you're ready?

00:07:41.493 --> 00:07:42.929
- Yes, sir.
- Let's do this.

00:07:48.301 --> 00:07:52.472
[music playing]

00:08:00.579 --> 00:08:01.613
 All right, Steve.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.482
Let's talk about
your blade here.

00:08:03.549 --> 00:08:06.585
First up, it's a heavy
blade that you got here.

00:08:06.685 --> 00:08:10.489
But your edge here, sir,
easy to cut and wield.

00:08:10.589 --> 00:08:13.125
Overall, sir, it will cut.

00:08:13.192 --> 00:08:15.495
 Thank you, sir.

00:08:15.561 --> 00:08:17.096
GRADY POWELL: Well, gentlemen,
you both broke the curse

00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:19.532
with your boneyard blades,
but only one of you guys

00:08:19.599 --> 00:08:21.667
can move forward in the next
round of this tournament.

00:08:21.767 --> 00:08:24.036
And the bladesmith
leaving the forage is--

00:08:26.739 --> 00:08:28.007
Steve.

00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:29.742
Unfortunately, your blade just
didn't make the cut today.

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:31.643
I'm going to have to ask you
to step off the forge floor.

00:08:31.743 --> 00:08:33.145
 Thanks for having me, guys.

00:08:33.212 --> 00:08:34.213
Good job, buddy.

00:08:34.279 --> 00:08:34.946
 Nice to see you again, Steve.

00:08:35.013 --> 00:08:35.748
 Thanks.

00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:37.616
STEVE: I left my
blade too heavy.

00:08:37.716 --> 00:08:40.453
You know, I was erring
on the side of caution,

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:43.623
making it good and stout,
and I just overdid it.

00:08:43.689 --> 00:08:46.558
I just want people to know that
this is a tough competition.

00:08:46.658 --> 00:08:51.063
And if you think it's
not, come and try.

00:08:51.129 --> 00:08:53.231
GRADY POWELL: Well, James,
you know what that means.

00:08:53.298 --> 00:08:54.699
You have won your duel.

00:08:54.766 --> 00:08:57.203
You're going to be
receiving a check for $2,000

00:08:57.269 --> 00:09:00.138
and you've got yourself a
slot in the final round of

00:09:00.238 --> 00:09:01.473
our second chance tournament.

00:09:01.574 --> 00:09:02.841
Congratulations.

00:09:02.941 --> 00:09:04.343
 Thank you.

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:05.410
GRADY POWELL: Very
well done, man.

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:06.879
JAMES: Thank you.

00:09:06.946 --> 00:09:09.115
Woo!

00:09:09.181 --> 00:09:12.384
I am just in utter astonishment.

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:14.953
I cannot believe what happened.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:17.023
To make it all the way
is just unbelievable.

00:09:17.123 --> 00:09:19.825
Steve was such an
awesome competitor.

00:09:19.892 --> 00:09:21.260
I know that this
is not over yet.

00:09:21.327 --> 00:09:24.229
I know there's three other
smiths there that have just

00:09:24.296 --> 00:09:26.832
as much want and drive and
desire to be "Forged in Fire"

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:30.770
champions as me, so I've
got a long road ahead.

